    "lawCode": "Amd §1891, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A10955\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public authorities law, in\nrelation to distribution utility\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:  To expand the definition of\ndistribution utility to include a rural electric cooperative, thereby\nallowing them to participate in the Green Jobs Green New York program\nestablished in 2009 and administered by the New York State Energy\nResearch and Development Authority (NYSERDA) with a goal of making\n1,000,000 homes, businesses, and not-for-profits in New York more\nenergy efficient while creating thousands of new green jobs and\ntraining the workers to fill them.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS: Section one:\n\nAmends the definition of distribution utility to include rural\nelectric cooperative.\n\nSection 2. This act shall take effect immediately.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  Green Jobs-Green New York promotes the widespread\ndissemination of energy conservation and clean energy technologies,\nproviding a clear and cost-effective pathway for communities in New\nYork State to curtail emissions of greenhouse gases and harmful air\ncontaminants, reduce dependence on fossil fuels, lower housing costs,\nsupport community development and create green jobs to sustain and\nenhance our economy.\n\nRural Electric Cooperatives were established in the early 1940's to\nprovide electric service to rural areas. They serve over 70,000 New\nYork residents and businesses throughout Cattaraugus, Chautauqua,\nChenango, Delaware, Herkimer, Madison, Oneida, Otsego, Schoharie and\nSteuben Counties.\n\nCurrently, this legislation provides competitive grants for outreach,\nenrollment and related services to implement this program. Eligible\nentities include distribution utilities, which as currently defined\nunder the Green Jobs Statute does not include Rural Electric\nCooperatives. This legislation merely adds rural electric cooperatives\nto the definition of distribution facility.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: 2010: New\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:  None\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:  This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
